[UK_AFR.FullName]
Last Step to Victory
[UK_AFR.Description]

[UK_AFR.Epilogue]
The squad of Major Richard Stevens blunted the enemy attack in its front sector, showing courage and heroism. Every soldier knew he may die in that battle but nobody was thinking of it. Their task was to resist and they did. Richard Stephens died heroically in battle on March 7, 1943. He lived a soldier’s life and died in combat, the way a true soldier should. He was promoted to Colonel posthumously.

‘Death is the beginning of another life’ M. Montaigne 

[Kasserine.FullName]
The Kasserine Pass
[Kasserine.Description]
\t Early in the morning of February 19, 1943, around 5:00 am, Major Richard Stevens was ordered to advance to the Kasserine pass and establish a defensive position. A great battle was coming. The Afrika Korps commanded by Erwin Rommel was rushing to Thala. Better trained than the Allies, the Germans were smaller in number. A U.S. unit commanded by Colonel Stark had already arrived at the pass. It was compulsory to establish a defensive position behind Stark's group by the evening of February 19, 1943. The high command believed that such a disposition would provide for the necessary depth of defence. The time to deploy was very limited, but they had to manage. Otherwise, a breach of Stark's line of defence would seriously threaten the Allies.
\t Having left his tent, major Stevens shivered. The air was damp, the wind was strong and it was drizzling ice-cold. Stevens issued the necessary orders and got back into the tent. It was warm here, firewood crackling in the stove. Memories rushed into his mind. Richard spent most of the childhood in India with his parents. His father was a career military serving in the colony. After moving back to England, it took a long time for Richard to get used to the new climate. He spent many free hours with a book by the fireplace. Having lived long among the military, he couldn't think of another profession. Having successfully graduated, he joined a military academy. At that time only service in the colonies could provide for a quick promotion. So, after graduating, 24-year old Richard Stevens left for the East. Palestine, Constantinople, Cyprus and, finally, Egypt. There he was caught by the war. The first weeks proved the incompetence of the British colonial troops, as it was the first war for most officers. But, accustomed to the hardships of colonial life, they quickly adapted to the new circumstances. The new style of warfare executed by the Germans was a tactical surprise for the British and all other armies that were opposing the new German war machine. The experience of defeat during the first months of war helped the Britons to work out new tactics, better suited for the new conditions. This was on the mind of Richard Stevens while he was looking at the fire burning in the stove.
One hour later the adjutant came in and reported that all orders had been executed and that the troops were already on the march. The Major asked for a breakfast. A toast, some porridge and tea, this set hadn't changed for nearly 10 years. Conservatism and something of a superiority complex were distinctive features of many British officers who served in the colonies. Having served many years away from home, they considered themselves professionals. U.S. personnel, from privates to generals, they considered green, inexperienced novices. So, at first there were serious misunderstandings in the Allied command. Some British officers ventured certain remarks regarding their American Allies that drew a wide response both among personnel and in Washington. The conflict might have grown to international dimensions but was suppressed in time.

[Thala.FullName]
On the Way to Thala
[Thala.Description]
\t Despite huge casualties, the British troops fiercely resisted the German forces. Rommel hadn't expected them to delay his army for that long at the Kasserine pass. Every hill was heavily fought for. German high command counted on the expertise of its troops and surprise. But having dragged out the start of the offensive, the Germans lost one of their trump cards - surprise. Rommel just had to hope for the expertise of his personnel. All day long he drove from one sector of the front to another, escorted by a driver and the adjutant, hurrying his military. As a result, before sunset of February 20, the Germans managed to secure the pass and expand the springboard for the further offensive. 
\t The group of Major Stevens suffered fewer casualties than the U.S. troops he was to support. It actually turned out there was hardly anyone to support. When  the Britons arrived, they barely had time to deploy. The Americans wavered and routed, but that wasn’t a big surprise. The British Allies were largely inexperienced, many didn't even know how to shoot. Most were torn from their peaceful lives just a couple of months prior, and officers were put in charge after a brief training. Nevertheless, there were some experienced commanders as well. They and their subordinates were to bear the main burden of enemy attacks. Richard Stevens met one of them when the Americans had retreated from their positions. It was Lieutenant Colonel Roger Waters. Despite his attitude towards the Americans, Major Stevens appreciated the man; and became even more convinced that he was right after the battle on February 20, when they had to repel attacking Germans together. In the evening they were ordered to withdraw, and only skillful cooperation of two groups helped to avoid heavy casualties during the retreat. 
\t In the night of February 20/21, Major Stevens invited Lieutenant Colonel Waters to dinner. They had a frank talk about the situation on the front. Both realized that the enemy had expertise and technical superiority on its side, and that high command was planning to equalize this by mere numbers. Stevens appropriately remembered a quotation by Confucius, "To lead uninstructed people to war is to throw them away." Waters agreed and cited Churchill, "Generals are always preparing for the last war." After dinner, the commanders devised a tactical plan for tomorrow, as it was clear that the enemy would continue the offensive in the morning.

[SidiNSir.FullName]
The Defence of Sidi N'Sir
[SidiNSir.Description]
\t After a chain of defeats, at the cost of huge casualties, the Allies have managed to stop the advance of Rommel's forces. Of course, one could say that the Germans had no reserves and that the high commands of the Axis countries were at some disagreement. Still, the fact remained: The Allies gained a moderate, yet very important victory. Having withdrawn to the Kasserine pass, Rommel hoped to join efforts with von Arnim and the Italians to quickly regain the situation and continue the offensive. But all of a sudden von Arnim obtained Kesselring's permission to advance in a completely another direction. According to his plan, the 5th Army was to advance in the northern part of Tunisia on February 26, 1943, from the coast to the Bu Aradah valley. And again Rommel obeyed. Meanwhile, von Arnim, had devised an operation called "Ochsenkopf". Its primary objective was to advance from Mateur to Beja via Sidi N'Sir. The offensive military strength included 77 tanks including 14 Tigers commanded by Colonel Rudolf Lang. 
\t The Allies had not expected such a kicker, but it went well enough. The first strike came at a rather experienced British unit, the 5th Battalion of the Royal Hampshire Regiment supported by a battery of the 172nd Field Artillery Regiment. The British held the line at Sidi N'Sir. It was a small defensive post made of several buildings and sheds adjacent to the highway on one side and a railroad on the other. At the same time, it was a critically important strong point that, if lost, would deprive the British of an important line of communication. Only the courage of the defenders prevented that the German troops, superior in number and military hardware, captured it right away. 
\t After it had been nearly eliminated, the group of Major Stevens was reformed, and the Major himself was put in charge of the Sidi N'Sir defenders on February 24. It didn't seem strange that, after losing almost all men of his unit in battle, the commander was assigned to such an important sector. Before February 26, this defensive post was considered relatively quiet, as no one was aware that the Germans had planned to attack it. Having arrived, Stevens immediately reorganized the typical pre-war defences. Having learned a lot the hard way, the Major understood that such weak disposition wouldn't do for the current circumstances. At 4 o'clock in the morning of February 26 Stevens received a report that his order had been carried out. He personally made sure of this. After the inspection, he walked up a small hill to watch the sunrise. It was so quiet that it was hard to believe that just in a few hours hell would break lose.

[Medenine.FullName]
The Medenine Defensive
[Medenine.Description]
\t Though Stevens's group had retreated from Sidi N'Sir, they still managed to stop the advance of the German troops. Moreover, the Allies had time to strengthen their defences and easily, with few casualties, repelled the ensuing attacks by an enemy weakened after the battle of Sidi N'Sir. During the following week, the high commands of the Axis countries were preparing the decisive offensive in the direction of Medenine-Metameur. Although this operation was top secret, its plan leaked to the Allied command. As it turned out later, it was a success of the British intelligence that by means of gambling had recruited an Italian officer from the enemy HQ. As a result, Montgomery, who was in charge of the Medenine defences, just had to align the British formations according to the planned actions of the Germans. Rommel planned to arrange a feint by means of the 90th Light Infantry Division, while focusing the main attack on the British flank. But Montgomery withdrew large forces from there to the main axis of the assault. Thus, the German offensive failed.  
\t For the battle of Sidi N'Sir, Major Stevens was promoted to Lieutenant Colonel. On March 4, he was informed about this by Montgomery who summoned him in person to thank for a good operation. He said, "Your unit will act on the hottest spot of the battlefield. And a lot will depend on you, Major, to reach our goal - victory! Prepare for the hardest battle of your life." Having said that, he let Stevens go. The entire next day he was preparing the battle plan, arranging reconnaissance, speaking to company commanders. By early March 6, everything had been arranged. At 6:00 Germans started the preparation fire.
